WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.083 --> 00:00:03.044
Have you seen this man? Probably.

00:00:03.044 --> 00:00:06.006
This is one of the most famous
police sketches ever made.

00:00:06.006 --> 00:00:09.509
And it's supposed to be
this guy, Ted Kaczynski, a.k.a.

00:00:09.509 --> 00:00:13.096
the Unabomber, an American terrorist
who mailed homemade explosives

00:00:13.096 --> 00:00:16.057
across the country for almost 20 years.

00:00:16.099 --> 00:00:18.935
Obviously, the sketch artist
didn't have much to work with.

00:00:18.935 --> 00:00:22.147
And the Unabomber left
no fingerprints or DNA.

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:24.733
So how was he finally caught?

00:00:24.733 --> 00:00:26.026
Linguistics.

00:00:26.026 --> 00:00:37.120
I'm Erica Brozovsky, Ph.D.,
And this is Otherwords.

00:00:37.203 --> 00:00:40.415
Otherwords.

00:00:40.498 --> 00:00:41.833
When the Unabomber sent

00:00:41.833 --> 00:00:47.589
a 35,000 word manifesto to the newspapers,
the FBI had forensic linguists

00:00:47.589 --> 00:00:51.634
James Fitzgerald and Roger Shuy
examine it for language clues.

00:00:51.718 --> 00:00:55.180
They found some outdated terminologies
like “broad” and “chick”

00:00:55.388 --> 00:00:59.559
that weren't likely to come from a woman
or a young person. Arcane words

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:03.980
like anemic and chimerical suggested
a high level of education,

00:01:04.064 --> 00:01:08.109
and they knew that odd spellings,
like “wilfully” and “clew” were briefly

00:01:08.109 --> 00:01:11.112
popular in the Chicago area
in the forties and fifties.

00:01:11.154 --> 00:01:14.657
In 1995,
Linda Patrick was reading the manifesto

00:01:14.657 --> 00:01:19.496
in the Washington Post
and found it disturbingly familiar.

00:01:19.579 --> 00:01:23.041
She asked her husband David to take a look
and he instantly recognized

00:01:23.041 --> 00:01:27.045
some of the phrases from letters
his brother had written to newspapers.

00:01:27.128 --> 00:01:30.298
At the time,
the FBI was receiving thousands of tips.

00:01:30.507 --> 00:01:33.551
But when they heard that David's
brother was a highly educated,

00:01:33.551 --> 00:01:37.222
middle aged man from the Chicago area,
it piqued their interest.

00:01:37.305 --> 00:01:39.224
Fitzgerald and Shuy compared the letters

00:01:39.224 --> 00:01:42.644
David supplied them with the manifesto
and concluded that

00:01:42.644 --> 00:01:46.606
unless it was an elaborate hoax,
this had to be their man.

00:01:46.689 --> 00:01:47.690
Shortly thereafter,

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:50.610
the FBI raided an isolated cabin
in the Montana

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:54.697
wilderness where they found Ted Kaczynski,
along with bomb-making materials

00:01:54.697 --> 00:01:57.867
and an original copy of the manifesto.

00:01:58.409 --> 00:02:01.412
In a way, we are all forensic linguists.

00:02:01.496 --> 00:02:03.915
Have you ever reread a friend's text

00:02:03.915 --> 00:02:07.460
trying to deduce
why they're really skipping your party?

00:02:07.544 --> 00:02:10.964
Have you ever pored over a warranty
before making a big purchase?

00:02:11.047 --> 00:02:14.801
Or stared at graffiti on a bathroom wall
trying to guess what

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:17.011
Jesse could have done
to get called out like that?

00:02:17.011 --> 00:02:20.765
In all of these cases, you're
analyzing linguistic clues like word

00:02:20.765 --> 00:02:24.811
choice, grammar and punctuation
to dig for hidden meaning.

00:02:24.894 --> 00:02:27.605
But the term forensic linguistics
didn't exist

00:02:27.605 --> 00:02:31.109
until the 1960s, when Swedish linguist
Jan Svartvik

00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:35.363
reexamined a controversial murder case
through a linguistic lens.

00:02:35.405 --> 00:02:40.493
In 1949, a Welshman named Timothy Evans
confessed to the strangling of his wife

00:02:40.493 --> 00:02:44.247
and baby daughter in a series of strangely
contradictory statements.

00:02:44.497 --> 00:02:48.334
Three years after his conviction
and hanging it was discovered that Evans’

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:52.964
downstairs neighbor, a man named John
Christie, was actually a serial killer

00:02:53.047 --> 00:02:56.009
and the real murderer of Evans’
wife and daughter.

00:02:56.009 --> 00:02:59.304
Why would Evans confess to something
he didn't do?

00:02:59.345 --> 00:03:01.389
Since he was functionally illiterate,

00:03:01.389 --> 00:03:04.225
all his statements had been dictated
to police investigators,

00:03:04.225 --> 00:03:07.228
totaling almost 5000 words.

00:03:07.353 --> 00:03:10.106
Svartvik
analyzed the text using several metrics,

00:03:10.106 --> 00:03:13.318
like the frequency of different types
of grammatical clauses.

00:03:13.401 --> 00:03:16.988
What he found was that whenever
the statements veered into fantasy,

00:03:16.988 --> 00:03:20.241
a different voice seemed to take over.

00:03:20.325 --> 00:03:23.328
Maybe Evans changed his speaking style
when he lied.

00:03:23.328 --> 00:03:28.166
Or maybe the dictating officer
took some creative liberties.

00:03:28.208 --> 00:03:31.211
This kind of analysis, known as stylometry,

00:03:31.211 --> 00:03:34.047
assumes that each of us has our own idiolect,

00:03:34.047 --> 00:03:38.718
a unique way of speaking that can be used
like a fingerprint to identify us.

00:03:38.801 --> 00:03:41.721
Your idiolect includes
obvious things like vocabulary

00:03:41.721 --> 00:03:45.683
and pronunciation,
but also subtle syntactic habits.

00:03:45.725 --> 00:03:48.937
Do you prefer active voice
or passive voice?

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:53.316
Do you tend to put adverbial clauses
before or after the main clause?

00:03:53.399 --> 00:03:56.569
You may not notice these habits,
but a forensic linguist might.

00:03:56.778 --> 00:04:01.574
One of the key methods of stylometry
is categorizing words and counting them.

00:04:01.658 --> 00:04:06.037
People who use more nouns, articles
and prepositions tend to be more rational

00:04:06.037 --> 00:04:08.206
and analytic, while those who favor

00:04:08.206 --> 00:04:12.252
verbs, adverbs and pronouns
are more dynamic and emotional.

00:04:12.335 --> 00:04:15.255
You can also put words
into broad thematic categories

00:04:15.255 --> 00:04:18.549
like family, religion, sensuality
and death.

00:04:18.633 --> 00:04:22.595
In 2015, social psychologist
Ryan L Boyd and linguist

00:04:22.595 --> 00:04:25.765
James W Pennebaker used this word
counting method

00:04:25.765 --> 00:04:29.477
to analyze Shakespeare's plays
and build a psychological profile,

00:04:29.644 --> 00:04:32.981
Much like an FBI agent
would for a criminal. They compared it

00:04:32.981 --> 00:04:36.276
to the text of a play with disputed
authorship called Double Falsehood,

00:04:36.484 --> 00:04:40.863
and found that Shakespeare was the most
likely author of the first three acts,

00:04:41.030 --> 00:04:44.659
with his frequent collaborator
John Fletcher writing the last two.

00:04:44.659 --> 00:04:47.996
Stylometry has been used to identify
modern authors as well.

00:04:48.079 --> 00:04:52.166
It's how we know that the Zapatista
military leader, Subcomandante Marcos,

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:56.379
is actually the respected writer
Rafael Sebastián Guillén Vicente.

00:04:56.462 --> 00:04:59.841
It's how journalist Joe Klein was outed
as the anonymous author

00:04:59.841 --> 00:05:04.679
of the political novel Primary Colors
and how whodunit scribe Robert Galbraith

00:05:04.846 --> 00:05:08.766
was revealed to be a pseudonym
for Hogwarts billionaire J.K. Rowling.

00:05:08.766 --> 00:05:11.769
But authorship attribution
is a lot easier

00:05:11.769 --> 00:05:15.440
when you have a big body of text
to analyze, known as a corpus.

00:05:15.523 --> 00:05:18.860
In criminal investigations, it's
not every day that the perpetrator sends

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:21.863
you a 35,000 word manifesto.

00:05:21.863 --> 00:05:23.406
Smooth move, Ted.

00:05:23.406 --> 00:05:26.409
Most of the time
you're working with much smaller samples.

00:05:26.534 --> 00:05:31.289
Take the 1996 murder of six year old
pageant queen JonBenet Ramsey.

00:05:31.372 --> 00:05:34.876
Even before her body was discovered
in the basement of the family home,

00:05:35.084 --> 00:05:38.171
investigators already thought
that there was something hinky

00:05:38.171 --> 00:05:40.089
about the ransom note left behind.

00:05:40.089 --> 00:05:44.635
For one thing, it was about three times
longer than an average ransom note,

00:05:44.844 --> 00:05:47.680
with phrases lifted
right out of popular action movies.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:50.266
“Do not attempt to grow a brain.”

00:05:50.266 --> 00:05:53.770
The letter also contained
several instances of what linguists call

00:05:53.770 --> 00:05:55.563
“adversarial stylometry,”

00:05:55.563 --> 00:05:58.566
when someone deliberately tries
to obscure their writing style,

00:05:58.691 --> 00:06:01.694
like misspelling simple words
or claiming to be foreign,

00:06:01.778 --> 00:06:04.989
which is not an adjective that people
often use to describe themselves.

00:06:05.114 --> 00:06:09.285
Linguists are also called in to analyze
letters sent to the police by supposed

00:06:09.285 --> 00:06:12.705
serial killers like the Zodiac Killer
and the Beltway Sniper.

00:06:12.872 --> 00:06:17.335
In 2018, a forensic linguist
from the University of Manchester

00:06:17.377 --> 00:06:21.005
used modern techniques
to analyze the letters sent to London

00:06:21.005 --> 00:06:25.885
police in 1888
by someone claiming to be Jack the Ripper.

00:06:25.927 --> 00:06:27.470
Not only did he confirm that

00:06:27.470 --> 00:06:31.391
the first two letters were written
by the same person, but also found

00:06:31.391 --> 00:06:35.561
a potential connection with a third letter
that was long thought to be a hoax

00:06:35.812 --> 00:06:39.565
written by someone from the Central News
Agency to boost newspaper sales.

00:06:39.774 --> 00:06:43.986
This might mean that all the letters
were hoaxes, that the real killer

00:06:43.986 --> 00:06:48.866
never wrote to the police at all
and never named himself Jack the Ripper.

00:06:48.950 --> 00:06:53.204
In such cases, the corpus is so small
that they can only make guesses

00:06:53.204 --> 00:06:57.333
about the type of person who wrote them
rather than match it to a specific author.

00:06:57.417 --> 00:07:01.754
But that may be changing thanks to
technology. In the era of social media,

00:07:02.004 --> 00:07:05.007
aach one of us has an extensive corpus
of posts,

00:07:05.174 --> 00:07:09.262
tweets and comments that can be mined
to build an idiolectic profile.

00:07:09.345 --> 00:07:13.641
And artificial intelligence can analyze
that corpus with stunning accuracy.

00:07:13.724 --> 00:07:17.311
After all, that's exactly
what these algorithms were designed to do:

00:07:17.395 --> 00:07:19.313
find patterns in language.

00:07:19.313 --> 00:07:24.068
But the complexity of AI can also limit
its usefulness in forensic linguistics.

00:07:24.152 --> 00:07:27.405
The most powerful systems
are typically unsupervised,

00:07:27.488 --> 00:07:31.075
meaning their own programmers don't know
how they reach their conclusions.

00:07:31.159 --> 00:07:35.037
They find patterns in language
so obscure that a professional linguist

00:07:35.037 --> 00:07:38.499
would have trouble understanding it,
let alone a jury of laypeople.

00:07:38.541 --> 00:07:41.002
And that does not fly in a court of law.

00:07:41.002 --> 00:07:45.089
You can't have a computer on the witness
stand just say “Trust me, he's your guy.”

00:07:45.173 --> 00:07:46.382
Perhaps the day will come

00:07:46.382 --> 00:07:50.386
when A.I. evidence is as accepted
and normalized as DNA evidence.

00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:54.891
But until then, juries can't convict
someone based on the word of an algorithm.

00:07:54.974 --> 00:07:57.894
Unless we outsource jury duty to A.I. as well,

00:07:57.894 --> 00:08:01.147
and I'm terrified to think
how many people would be okay with that.

00:08:01.230 --> 00:08:04.817
For now, human
linguists still have a place in courtrooms

00:08:04.817 --> 00:08:07.028
and not just to introduce evidence.

00:08:07.028 --> 00:08:09.822
They help lawyers
fine tune their arguments, parse

00:08:09.822 --> 00:08:12.200
complicated statements with disputed
meanings,

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:15.953
monitor police interrogation tactics,
and even ensure that non-English

00:08:15.953 --> 00:08:18.956
speaking defendants are interpreted
fairly and accurately.

00:08:19.123 --> 00:08:23.461
The fact that we all have our own unique
way of speaking is a beautiful thing.

00:08:23.544 --> 00:08:25.963
It's at the core of personal expression

00:08:25.963 --> 00:08:28.966
and contributes
a wonderful tapestry of language.

00:08:29.175 --> 00:08:34.055
But it also means that no form
of expression can be 100% anonymous.

00:08:34.138 --> 00:08:36.557
As the famous Miranda warning goes,

00:08:36.557 --> 00:08:41.354
anything you say can and will be
used against you in a court of law.

00:08:42.605 --> 00:08:43.898
Styl--stylometry

00:08:43.898 --> 00:08:46.150
Stylometry, yeah.

00:08:46.150 --> 00:08:49.820
Every time I say it it feels weird. Stylometry. Drama!

00:08:49.904 --> 00:08:51.572
Jan? John? Jan.

00:08:51.572 --> 00:08:53.449
Jan Svartvik.

00:08:53.449 --> 00:08:56.410
Did you hear me say often? Often with a T.

